OK  | url with no value | args=["--relay-url"] | exit=2 | stderr="coagentd: --relay-url needs a value\nusage:\n  coagentd --data-dir <dir> [--coagent <path>] [--relay-url <wss://...> --relay-pin <hex>]\n           [--idle-exit-secs <n>] [--name <display name>] [--keep-relay-identity]\n           [--gate --workspace <dir> [--workspace <dir>...] [--exclude <glob>...] [--gate-dir <dir>...]]\n  coagentd ctl --data-dir <dir> '<json request line>'\n  coagentd watch --data-dir <dir>   (the gate's decisions on this machine, for your eyes only)\n\n  The relay this device runs for a session it starts has a TLS identity (its certificate\n  fingerprint is the pin in the invitation). Default: a new identity for each session, kept in\n  memory only, with nothing on disk; if the relay restarts within the session it gets the same\n  identity, so the other side reconnects by itself. After a restart of coagentd itself the other\n  side needs a new line (the relay.moved line in status).\n  --keep-relay-identity  keep the identity in <dir>/relay-identity.key (created mode 0600 if\n        absent, loaded if present), across sessions and restarts, so the other side reconnects\n        by itself after a restart of coagentd too (if the relay gets its old address back).\n        A file that fails a check is refused: the relay is not started and no new identity is\n        made until the file is put right or moved away. Not supported on Windows yet.\n        What is checked: the owner and the permission bits of the file and of the data\n        directory. NOT checked: access-control lists (ACLs) and other extended sharing rules\n        on macOS, and folders above the data directory. Any program running as the same user\n        can read the file. Every invitation from this data directory then shows the same\n        fingerprint, so anyone who has seen two of them can tell they are the same relay.\n        Whoever copies the file, or reads this program's memory, can stand where the relay\n        stands: they see who connects, when, and how much, and can drop traffic; they cannot\n        read content or pair.\n\n  On Windows:\n  The data folder must be a full path (a drive letter, ':' and a separator) inside your user\n  profile folder (USERPROFILE); a relative path is refused. Windows: the data folder is inside\n  your user profile folder (USERPROFILE), and that is the only check. NOT checked: who owns the\n  folder, its access list, or whether Windows keeps other users out of your profile. For your own\n  machine only. --gate and --keep-relay-identity are refused. This build cannot host a relay:\n  start with --relay-url and --relay-pin for a relay hosted elsewhere, or join with the other\n  side's line. Ctrl-C stops it cleanly; closing its window does not (that is the crash path; not\n  run on Windows).\n  In the Command Prompt a ctl request is the JSON in double quotes, each inner \" written \\\":\n  coagentd ctl --data-dir <dir> \"{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"cmd\\\":\\\"status\\\"}\"" | created=False
